        'transcript' => "<p>This week, India has a hydrogen plant running on heat from a nuclear reactor. Two yacht builders are solving the same hydrogen storage problem in completely different ways. And a Chinese train manufacturer unveiled a new hydrogen train platform built for some of the toughest freight corridors on Earth.</p><p>Hi, I'm Steve Hill, CEO of NewHydrogen with this week's top hydrogen news. Before we get to the news, here's a quick update on our own progress. Our ThermoLoop engineering test unit is under construction and we're working with NuCube Energy to evaluate using heat from their next generation nuclear reactor to power ThermoLoop and lower the cost of producing clean hydrogen. Please subscribe if you'd like to follow our progress.</p><p>First up, India is running a hydrogen power plant powered entirely by heat from a nuclear reactor built at a national research center developing a copper chlorine production process. It's a serious milestone for a country with enormous energy demand but limited domestic fuel supply.</p><p>But the process still includes an electrolysis step using an electrolyzer, the same decades-old expensive technology that's kept hydrogen costs high everywhere else. Even a government-funded flagship program hasn't found a way around the cost driver that's held clean hydrogen back for decades.</p><p>Next, two yacht builders are tackling the same challenge and came up with two clever, completely different answers. The question: how do you get hydrogen fuel cell power into a boat?</p><p>Feed Ships Project 821, a 118-meter super yacht, stores liquid hydrogen directly on board. San Lorenzo, the parent company behind Blue Game, skipped storing hydrogen altogether on its 50 Steel, carrying green methanol instead and reforming it into hydrogen on demand right before it reaches the fuel cell in a system built with Siemens Energy.</p><p>Two different engineering paths, same destination — a boat running on clean hydrogen power. That