s largest rail operators sees hydrogen as the practical path where overhead electrification isn't cost-effective. If the pilot succeeds, the door to trunk routes serving major cities opens quickly.</p><p>Next, hydrogen is doing something most people would never think about. It helps feed the world. Siemens and Shougang have announced a collaboration to build new United States production facilities for Nitro Leaf, a zero-carbon green ammonia fertilizer, at a time when global supply chains are under pressure and farmer input costs are rising sharply. Here's why that matters. Roughly 50% of the world's population depends on food grown with synthetic fertilizers.</p><p>Making fertilizer requires ammonia. Making ammonia requires hydrogen. Today, almost all of it comes from fossil fuels. Projects like this replace that with clean hydrogen, cutting agricultural's carbon footprint at a scale that directly affects food security. Hydrogen isn't just an energy story. It's woven into the basic infrastructure of how the world eats.</p><p>Finally, hydrogen trucking in America just got the most credible commercial signal yet. Hyundai Translead is shipping up to 100 XCIENT Class 8 hydrogen fuel cell tractors to the United States, making Hyundai currently one of the only suppliers of Class 8 hydrogen trucks in North America.</p><p>Rivals, including Kenworth and Peterbilt, have faced delays and production setbacks, leaving Hyundai in a wide-open commercial field. The XCIENT trucks are produced in South Korea and will be distributed through Hyundai's dealer network as the company builds out infrastructure support ahead of broader growth after 2027. Class 8 trucks cover long distances and can't afford hours of charging downtime. Hydrogen's fast refueling and range make it a natural fit.</p><p>Hyundai shipping real trucks to real customers while competitors are still calibrating puts them in a strong early position. From a nuclear-heated hydrogen process to a train in India to American 