        'transcript' => "<p>Porsche just found a way to make cars generate their own fuel while they drive, and it's part of a global shift that's fundamentally changing how we power hospitals and even how we feed the planet. Hi, I'm Steve Hill, CEO of New Hydrogen, with this week's top hydrogen news. Can you imagine a high-performance engine that actually produces its own fuel? A new patent from Porsche suggests they are working on exactly that.</p><p>According to reports from CarBuzz, Porsche has designed a system that carries specialized water-splitting hardware right on the chassis to generate hydrogen gas on demand. The water can even come from the car's windshield washer reservoir. Now, this isn't about replacing the gas tank. Porsche's goal is to solve the cold start problem. By injecting hydrogen during startup, they can preheat the catalytic converter instantly, eliminating the dirtiest part of the combustion cycle.</p><p>While this onboard approach works for a single car, it highlights a bigger truth. The future of combustion depends on having hydrogen available whenever and wherever you need it. It's a brilliant example of a legendary brand using hydrogen as a high-tech tool to keep the soul of the internal combustion engine alive. Porsche is joining a massive wave of hydrogen engine development from the world's most iconic brands.</p><p>Toyota is currently testing a 400 horsepower hydrogen-capable engine, and they've teamed up with Yamaha, Kawasaki, Subaru, and Mazda to ensure the roar of the internal combustion engine has a place in a zero-emission future.</p><p>Ferrari has also joined the race, recently patenting a radical upside-down hydrogen engine designed to maximize space for fuel storage. And Hyundai, already a global leader with nearly 30 years of hydrogen fuel cell experience, is now expanding its dominance into hydrogen combustion engines for heavy-duty equipment and commercial vehicles. It's a brilliant collective effort to use hydrogen as a 