y, we turn to a development in the global energy market that could dramatically increase the cost of hydrogen. According to Gasworld, the return of heavy Venezuelan crude oil to US Gulf Coast refineries is tightening the domestic hydrogen market.</p><p>Unlike lighter crude, this heavy and sour oil contains higher levels of sulfur and other impurities. To turn it into usable fuels like gasoline, diesel and jet fuel, refineries have to run it through intensive upgrading and desulerization processes. And those processes consume a lot of hydrogen. Many refineries already produce hydrogen on site, but when processing heavier crude, their demand can spike significantly. That forces some operators to pull more hydrogen from the merchant market, leaving less available for other industries that depend on it.</p><p>It's a reminder that hydrogen is more than just a future energy carrier. It's a critical industrial commodity. And when hydrogen prices rise, the effects ripple through industries, even increasing the cost of fertilizers that are essential for growing the food the world needs to feed the world. From powering an 800 horsepower supercar to cleaning up port trucking and supporting large-scale refining, these stories all point to the same conclusion.</p><p>The world needs cheap, clean hydrogen. And that's why at New Hydrogen, we're developing Thermal Loop, a technology designed to use cheap heat instead of expensive electricity to split water with the goal of producing the world's cheapest clean hydrogen. If you'd like to see how it works,</p><p>Visit us at newhydrogen.com and watch our short explainer video. New Hydrogen is a publicly traded company under the symbol NEWH. Thanks for your support and we'll see you again next week.</p>"
