on. Now, let's talk about Ferrari because this story made headlines for a reason. We just learned that Ferrari's new hydrogen combustion engine can run upside down. Not figuratively, literally upside down. The patent describes a hydrogen engine design that doesn't rely on traditional oil circulation.</p><p>Instead, it uses a sealed lubrication system that works at any angle, even during extreme track maneuvers. Ferrari is not replacing oil completely, but this approach allows the engine to operate under conditions that would choke a normal powertrain. Even better, it makes space for a hydrogen fuel cell under the hood. If the world's most profitable car company is seriously exploring hydrogen combustion, you know this technology isn't just a fantasy.</p><p>Hydrogen is moving fast, and so are we at New Hydrogen. To see our progress and how our thermaloupe technology could help supply the cheap, clean hydrogen these industries need, visit newhydrogen.com. That's it for this week's news. Thanks for watching and we'll see you again next week.</p>"
