n hydrogen, unlocking what Goldman Sachs has called a 12 trillion global market. Back in the United States, hydrogen has arrived on rails. In California's San Bernardino County, America's first hydrogen-powered passenger train has entered service.</p><p>Built by Swiss manufacturer Stadler, the ZEMU train runs on fuel cells that generate electricity on board, giving it a range of about 285 miles per refueling. The county expects it to carry more than a million passengers a year, replacing diesel-powered service with quieter zero emission travel. Meanwhile, the United Kingdom is putting big money into clean shipping. Nearly \$500 million has been pledged to develop next generation marine technology with hydrogen-based fuels taking a central role.</p><p>The funding will support new vessels, port infrastructure, and demonstration projects aimed at decarbonizing one of the hardest to abate sectors. For an island nation where shipping is vital, hydrogen-based fuels are a practical pathway to cleaner trade and transport. And</p><p>Finally, the global view. The International Energy Agency released its 2025 global hydrogen review, showing that a pipeline of announced projects has grown to cover nearly every major sector from power and industry to aviation and heavy transport. The report also makes clear that cost remains the key challenge, noting that cheap, clean hydrogen is the single factor that will determine how quickly these projects move from announcement to reality.</p><p>Hydrogen is advancing on every front, aviation, rail, shipping, and global supply. But none of this innovation works without cheap, clean hydrogen. That's the future we're building right now at New Hydrogen. To see the future of hydrogen production today, visit newhydrogen.com to watch our benchtop demo and our short explainer video. Thanks for watching and I'll see you again next week.</p>"
