r platform for racing teams and car makers looking to cut emissions without switching to batteries or fuel cells.</p><p>Last up, a hydrogen first in rail. Austria has installed the world's first railway tracks made from hydrogen reduced steel produced by Fullstein using green hydrogen instead of coal. This lowcarbon steel is being tested on a 6 km stretch of track near Lintz. This trial is a major step toward decarbonizing one of the most emission intensive industries in the world, steel making, while supporting cleaner infrastructure for the rail industry itself.</p><p>That's it for this week's news. To see what hydrogen can really do and where it's headed next, visit us at newhydrogen.com and check out our live thermal loop demo and explainer video. Thanks for watching and we'll see you again next week.</p>"
