        'transcript' => "<p>When the world's most advanced economies, automakers, and aerospace leaders all double down on the same thing in the same week, it's time to pay attention. Hi, I'm Steve Hill, CEO of New Hydrogen, with this week's top hydrogen news. First up, Japan is making its intentions clear. Japan has now committed over \$100 billion through 2050 to scale up its green hydrogen economy. That includes domestic production, import infrastructure, and widespread integration across power, transport, and industry. Japan's government isn't just interested in hydrogen. It's prioritizing it as a national strategy.</p><p>Ministries are aligning policy. Utilities are developing pilot plants, and heavy industry is preparing to adopt hydrogen-based systems. They're even building entire hydrogen cities from scratch. This is not a theoretical road map. Japan is moving forward and with real urgency. Next up, Hyundai is backing hydrogen innovation with real capital. The company just launched a \$93 million venture fund targeting earlystage startups. Six of the 11 companies selected are developing hydrogen related technologies, including advanced materials, storage systems, and fuel cells. What makes this move especially impactful is Hyundai's broader support.</p><p>It's offering these startups access to in-house labs, testing facilities, and pilot platforms. It's a strategic way to accelerate the path from concept to deployment and a strong signal that hydrogen remains central to Hyundai's long-term strategy. Last item, hydrogen aviation is entering a decade of growth.</p><p>A new market outlook from Polaris Market Research projects the global hydrogen aircraft sector to grow at over 20% annually, reaching a value of 42 billion by 2034. Behind those numbers is a growing push to decarbonize air travel. Major players like Airbus, Rolls-Royce, and ZeroAV are developing both hydrogen combustion engines and fuel cell propulsion systems. Support from regulatory agencies and